In this lesson we'll look at how to tell when data is following a quadratic model, and how to pick the right shape for the job. So, linear models handle things that change at a steady rate, like adding the same amount every step. But when the rate of change itself is changing, you need a different shape, and that's where quadratics come in. A quantity is growing quadratically when its second differences are constant — meaning the change between the changes stays the same. Think of the area of a square as the side gets longer, or the distance an object falls under gravity, both of those grow quadratically. A revenue example works too: if the quantity sold drops steadily as the price rises, then revenue ends up being quadratic in price. To recognise a quadratic, take first differences, then take differences of those, and if those second differences are constant, you've got a quadratic. Choosing between models comes down to one question: what is constant? A constant amount added each step is linear. A constant multiplier each step is exponential. A constant change in the change is quadratic. Real data often sits between simple models, so digital tools help you fit a curve and check the match. Now let's see it in action.